import <wdoc.wcl>

concept why_use_wskill {
    name    = "Why use wskill"
    summary = "Capture a topic once, as self-contained data, and get both a readable book and a Claude Code skill from the same source — kept honest as the topic changes."
    related = [wskill_concept, selfcontained, book_view, skill_view]
    body {
        p <<BODY
            A wskill earns its place when one topic has to serve more than one audience and outlive
            more than one sitting. Instead of writing a wiki page for people and a separate prompt
            for an AI — and watching the two drift apart — you capture the topic once as structured
            data and let the templates project it. The same source becomes a human-readable book
            and a Claude Code skill, so there is a single thing to write, review, and correct.
        BODY

        list {
            li "**Shareable.** A wskill is a self-contained folder — hand it to a person or an agent and nothing else is needed to use it."
            li "**One source of truth.** Reference, processes, and curated navigation live as data, so the book and the skill can never disagree."
            li "**Built to learn from.** Concepts, entities, facts, and processes are atomic and cross-linked, so a reader (or a model) can pick the topic up from the package alone."
            li "**Easy to keep current.** When the topic changes you edit the data once; every projection re-renders, and the schema fact reflects the live schema so the reference can't go stale."
        }
    }
}
